From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests: skip the live-github.com-dependent cert chain test

tests/cert_chain_verifier_test.py::TestCertificateChainVerifier::
test_valid_certificate_chain's certificate_chain_as_x509 fixture does not
use a bundled fixture chain: it calls ssl.get_server_certificate(
("www.github.com", 443)) to fetch whatever leaf certificate GitHub is
serving right now, then pairs that live leaf with a hardcoded PEM for
"Sectigo ECC Domain Validation Secure Server CA" (captured 2018-11-02,
per the cert's own notBefore) as the "intermediate", and asks nassl's
CertificateChainVerifier to build a chain from those two up to a root in
the bundled tests/mozilla.pem trust store.

This reproduced as a deterministic failure - not a flake - identically on
both the cp313 and cp314 legs of this riscv64 build:

    nassl.cert_chain_verifier.CertificateChainVerificationFailed:
    Verification failed with OpenSSL error code 20: "unable to get local
    issuer certificate"

Root cause has nothing to do with riscv64, this build, or nassl's
statically-linked OpenSSL_1_1_1w:

- mozilla.pem is present and loads correctly (the test gets as far as a
  real OpenSSL chain-validation error, not a file/collection error), so
  this is not a staged-fixture/packaging problem.
- CertificateChainVerifier.verify() builds the chain purely from the PEM
  bytes it is handed (X509_STORE_CTX.set0_trusted_stack/set0_untrusted
  over in-memory certs); it never touches the system or build's default
  CA path, so no OpenSSL build flag affecting CA-path defaults is
  involved either.
- ssl.get_server_certificate() goes through Python's stdlib ssl module
  (the runner's/manylinux image's system OpenSSL), not nassl's own
  bundled OpenSSL_1_1_1w, and it is that *live* leaf, not a stale one,
  that fails to chain.
- What has actually changed is GitHub's own serving CA: as of this run,
  www.github.com's ECDSA leaf is issued by "Sectigo Public Server
  Authentication CA DV E36", a newer Sectigo intermediate generation
  that replaced "Sectigo ECC Domain Validation Secure Server CA" since
  this test's fixture was written. The hardcoded intermediate's subject
  no longer matches the live leaf's issuer at all, so OpenSSL can't find
  an issuer for the leaf - exactly what error 20 means - regardless of
  interpreter, architecture, or OpenSSL build.
- This is a known failure mode of this exact test, not introduced by
  this port: nassl's Kali Linux packaging has separately hit this same
  test failing from the same live-fetch-vs-hardcoded-cert design, there
  with error 10 ("certificate has expired") instead of error 20 - a
  different symptom of the same root cause (an external, live TLS
  endpoint checked against certificate material frozen in source at
  some point in the past).
- The sibling test, test_expired_certificate_chain, uses a fully static,
  self-contained (if actually expired) chain and is unaffected; only the
  live-fetch-dependent happy-path test breaks under CA rotation.

Since GitHub will keep rotating its serving CA on its own schedule,
hardcoding today's correct intermediate would only move the next break
further out, not fix the underlying fragility - so skip the test rather
than re-pin another intermediate that will go stale again.

Upstream-Status: To upstream [not yet submitted; the live-github.com dependency and the hardcoded intermediate are both upstream's own test design, not anything riscv64/RISE-specific, but no PR has been opened yet]

Signed-off-by: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
---
 tests/cert_chain_verifier_test.py | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/tests/cert_chain_verifier_test.py b/tests/cert_chain_verifier_test.py
index cd561bc..4160645 100644
--- a/tests/cert_chain_verifier_test.py
+++ b/tests/cert_chain_verifier_test.py
@@ -43,6 +43,14 @@ cS0chWBNKPFNpvDKdrdKRe+oAkr2jU+ubgIxAODheSr2XhcA7oz9HmedGdMhlrd9


 class TestCertificateChainVerifier:
+    @pytest.mark.skip(
+        reason="certificate_chain_as_x509 hardcodes the 2018-era 'Sectigo ECC Domain "
+        "Validation Secure Server CA' intermediate and chains it against a live leaf "
+        "fetched from www.github.com:443 at test time; github.com's real issuing CA has "
+        "since rotated (now 'Sectigo Public Server Authentication CA DV E36'), so the "
+        "hardcoded intermediate no longer matches the live leaf's issuer and chain "
+        "building fails with OpenSSL error 20 on any platform, not just riscv64."
+    )
     def test_valid_certificate_chain(self, certificate_chain_as_x509: list[X509]) -> None:
         path_validator = CertificateChainVerifier.from_file(Path(__file__).absolute().parent / "mozilla.pem")
         path_validator.verify(certificate_chain_as_x509)
--
2.43.0
